  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Saleem Khan vs State on 19 July, 2017",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "A latter judgment of the Supreme Court reported as 2004(5) SCC 721, Dayal Singh V/s State of Rajasthan differed from the law laid down in N.Sukumaran Nair's case. In a third decision reported as 2009(12) SCC 646, State of Rajasthan V/s Jagdish Prasad noting that the offence of adulteration though took place nearly three decades back, the Court directed that the sentence would remain suspended to enable the petitioner therein to file an application before the appropriate Government to exercise power under Section 433 of the Code of Criminal Procedure.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "A latter judgment of the Supreme Court reported as 2004(5) SCC 721, Dayal Singh V/s State of Rajasthan differed from the law laid down in N.Sukumaran Nair's case. In a third decision reported as 2009(12) SCC 646, State of Rajasthan V/s Jagdish Prasad noting that the offence of adulteration though took place nearly three decades back, the Court directed that the sentence would remain suspended to enable the petitioner therein to file an application before the appropriate Government to exercise power under Section 433 of the Code of Criminal Procedure."
